        {
          "subsection_id": "colostomy_bag_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "1. Introduction and definition",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "colostomy_bag_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A colostomy bag is a medical collection appliance attached to the abdominal wall over a surgically created opening of the colon called a colostomy. It collects faeces and flatus diverted from the normal anal route."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A colostomy may be temporary or permanent, depending on the underlying disease and the surgical procedure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Key identifying feature: A collection pouch with an adhesive skin barrier that fits around a stoma."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "In an OSCE, distinguish between:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Colostomy: The opening is formed from the colon.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Ileostomy: The opening is formed from the ileum.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Stoma: The surgically created opening of the bowel onto the skin.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Colostomy bag: The external appliance used to collect the output.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "colostomy_bag_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "2. Identification in an OSCE station",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "colostomy_bag_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/colostomy_bag_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Model identification"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“This is a colostomy bag, an ostomy appliance used to collect faeces from a surgically created colonic stoma.”",
                  "style": "quote"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Indications for its use"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "After bowel resection with stoma formation.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Following diversion of faeces away from a distal bowel segment.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "In selected colorectal malignancies, trauma, obstruction and inflammatory bowel disease.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "colostomy_bag_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "3. Parts and labelling",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "colostomy_bag_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/colostomy_bag_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "1. Collection pouch"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Collects faeces and flatus discharged from the stoma. It may be transparent, opaque, drainable or closed-ended."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/colostomy_bag_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "2. Skin barrier or wafer"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Adheres to the peristomal skin and protects it from bowel output. The opening must fit the stoma appropriately."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/colostomy_bag_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "3. Coupling ring or flange"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Present in two-piece systems; connects the pouch to the skin barrier."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/colostomy_bag_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "4. Outlet and closure"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Allows the contents to be emptied from a drainable pouch. The closure may use a clip or an integrated fastening mechanism."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/colostomy_bag_09.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "5. Gas filter (if present)"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Often contains charcoal to help deodorise gas and allow it to escape while reducing pouch ballooning."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Other possible components include a protective cover, adhesive border, convex skin barrier, belt attachment and accessories for protecting the peristomal skin."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "colostomy_bag_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "4. Classification and types",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "colostomy_bag_group_5",
              "group_title": "A. According to the number of components",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/colostomy_bag_10.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "One-piece system"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The pouch and skin barrier are integrated into one unit."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Simple to apply.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Low-profile and often flexible.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The entire appliance is changed when the barrier needs replacing.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/colostomy_bag_11.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Two-piece system"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The pouch attaches to a separate skin barrier."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "The pouch may be changed without removing the barrier.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Useful for patients who prefer less frequent removal of the adhesive barrier.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The coupling may add bulk.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "colostomy_bag_group_6",
              "group_title": "B. According to pouch design",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Type",
                    "Main feature",
                    "Typical use"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Closed-ended bag",
                      "Sealed bottom; discarded after use",
                      "Often suitable for formed, less frequent colostomy output"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Drainable bag",
                      "Outlet for emptying",
                      "Useful when output is frequent or relatively loose"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Transparent bag",
                      "Stoma and output can be inspected",
                      "Often helpful immediately after surgery"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Opaque bag",
                      "Conceals the contents",
                      "May be preferred for discretion"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Filtered bag",
                      "Has a gas-filtering system",
                      "Helps reduce gas accumulation and odour"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Convex appliance",
                      "Curved barrier designed to project toward the skin",
                      "Selected flush, retracted or difficult-to-seal stomas after appropriate assessment"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Exam point: The choice of appliance depends on the consistency and frequency of output, stoma shape, abdominal contour, skin condition, patient dexterity and preference."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "colostomy_bag_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "5. Indications and surgeries in which it is used",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "colostomy_bag_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A colostomy bag is used following operations that create a colostomy. The bag itself does not create the stoma."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "colostomy_bag_group_8",
              "group_title": "Common indications",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Colorectal cancer.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Large-bowel obstruction.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Colonic perforation and faecal contamination.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Complicated diverticular disease.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Selected cases of inflammatory bowel disease.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Severe colorectal trauma.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Protection of a distal bowel anastomosis in selected cases.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Congenital anorectal abnormalities in selected patients.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "colostomy_bag_group_9",
              "group_title": "Surgical procedures",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "1. Hartmann's procedure"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A diseased segment of colon, often the sigmoid colon, is resected; the distal bowel is closed, and the proximal colon is brought out as an end colostomy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "2. Abdominoperineal resection (APR)"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The rectum and anus are removed for selected low rectal or anal malignancies, resulting in a permanent end colostomy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "3. Loop colostomy formation"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A loop of colon is brought to the abdominal surface and opened to divert faeces. It may be temporary."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "4. End colostomy formation"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The proximal end of the colon is brought through the abdominal wall to form a stoma."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "5. Selected trauma and emergency colorectal operations"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A colostomy may be created when primary anastomosis is unsafe or diversion is clinically indicated."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "colostomy_bag_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "6. Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "colostomy_bag_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Diverts faeces away from diseased or injured distal bowel.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Can protect a selected distal anastomosis.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Allows faecal diversion in obstruction, perforation or trauma.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "May be lifesaving in emergency colorectal surgery.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Facilitates wound care where diversion is indicated.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Modern appliances support mobility, hygiene, dignity and social participation.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Some temporary colostomies can be reversed when the underlying condition and patient circumstances permit.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "colostomy_bag_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "7. Disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "colostomy_bag_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Requires regular emptying or replacement.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "May cause leakage and unpleasant odour.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Can irritate the surrounding skin.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "May affect body image, intimacy and psychological wellbeing.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Requires education and ongoing supplies.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Adhesive failure can occur due to perspiration, skin folds or poor fit.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Some patients experience difficulty managing the appliance independently.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "colostomy_bag_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "8. Complications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "colostomy_bag_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is important to distinguish complications of the bag and skin barrier from complications of the stoma or the surgery."
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Complication",
                    "Description"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Peristomal dermatitis",
                      "Irritation from faecal contact, adhesive products or moisture"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Leakage",
                      "Poor fit, barrier failure, folds or incorrect appliance selection"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Allergic contact dermatitis",
                      "Reaction to an adhesive or appliance component"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Mucocutaneous separation",
                      "Separation between the stoma and surrounding skin"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Stoma retraction",
                      "Stoma lies below the skin surface, making an effective seal difficult"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Stoma prolapse",
                      "Excessive protrusion of bowel through the stoma"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Parastomal hernia",
                      "Abdominal contents protrude around the stoma through a weakness in the abdominal wall"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Stoma stenosis",
                      "Narrowing of the stoma opening"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Ischaemia or necrosis",
                      "Compromised blood supply to the stoma; urgent assessment is required"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Bleeding",
                      "Minor contact bleeding may occur, but persistent or substantial bleeding needs assessment"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Red flags: A stoma that becomes dusky, purple, black or unusually pale; severe abdominal pain; persistent vomiting; absent output accompanied by pain or distension; or significant bleeding requires urgent clinical assessment."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "colostomy_bag_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "9. Principles of application and changing the bag",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "colostomy_bag_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "OSCE practical sequence — demonstrate hygiene, inspection, correct fitting and patient education."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 1 — Prepare"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Explain the procedure, obtain consent, provide privacy, perform hand hygiene and assemble the appliance, disposal bag, gauze, water or appropriate cleanser and measuring guide."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 2 — Remove the old appliance"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Gently support the skin while removing the barrier. Avoid forceful stripping of the adhesive."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 3 — Inspect the stoma and skin"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assess stoma colour, moisture, size, protrusion, output and the condition of the surrounding skin. Escalate abnormal colour or concerning findings."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 4 — Clean and dry"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Clean gently with warm water or an appropriate product according to local practice. Dry the skin thoroughly before applying the new barrier."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 5 — Measure and fit"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Measure the stoma and cut the barrier to fit closely without constricting it. Avoid leaving excessive exposed skin."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 6 — Apply and check"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Secure the barrier and pouch, ensure the outlet is closed if applicable, and check the seal. Empty a drainable pouch before it becomes excessively heavy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 7 — Document and educate"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Record relevant stoma and skin findings, output characteristics and any complications. Teach the patient emptying, changing, disposal and warning signs."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "colostomy_bag_group_14",
              "group_title": "Important practical points",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "The stoma should normally appear pink to red and moist.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The skin barrier opening should closely fit the stoma, taking into account its size and shape.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid routine use of oily creams beneath the adhesive because they can interfere with adhesion.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Change the appliance according to the product instructions, output, seal integrity and individual needs; do not rely on one universal changing interval.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A colostomy should not be irrigated routinely unless the patient has been assessed and specifically taught an appropriate irrigation regimen.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "colostomy_bag_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "10. Colostomy versus ileostomy: a common viva question",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "colostomy_bag_group_15",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Feature",
                    "Colostomy",
                    "Ileostomy"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Bowel segment",
                      "Colon",
                      "Ileum"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Typical output",
                      "Often thicker or more formed, depending on location",
                      "Usually looser and more liquid"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Fluid and electrolyte loss",
                      "Generally lower than with a high-output ileostomy",
                      "Greater risk, particularly with high output"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Common appliance",
                      "Closed-ended or drainable",
                      "Usually drainable"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Skin irritation risk",
                      "Present",
                      "Often greater due to liquid, enzyme-containing output"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Major ongoing concern",
                      "Appliance leakage, skin problems and stoma complications",
                      "Dehydration, electrolyte depletion and skin irritation"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "These are typical patterns, not absolute rules. A right-sided colostomy may produce relatively liquid output, while output from an ileostomy varies with intestinal function and diet."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
